# Keep the build context small and, more importantly, keep secrets and host-built artefacts out of it.
#
# `.env` is the line that matters most. Without it, a `COPY . .` in any future edit to the Dockerfile
# would bake real credentials into an image layer, where they survive deletion from the filesystem and
# travel with every `docker push`. The Dockerfile does not copy it today; this makes that safe rather
# than merely true.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Host-built output. These MUST be excluded rather than reused: dist/ and the generated Prisma clients
# are compiled on the developer's machine, and node_modules holds native modules (argon2) built for the
# host's platform and libc. Copying an arm64 macOS argon2 into a linux/amd64 image produces a binary
# that fails to load at require time, which is a confusing way to learn this lesson.
node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
**/src/generated
coverage

# Not needed at build or run time.
.git
.github
.claude
docs
*.md
!README.md
plan.md
problems.md
scripts/smoke-test.sh
scripts/slowloris-probe.mjs
scripts/verify-claims.mjs
docker-compose.yml
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
.nvmrc
.prettierrc
.prettierignore
eslint.config.mjs
vitest.config.ts
**/*.spec.ts
